@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Pull Requests and Code Reviews";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<style>
    .devops-img {
        max-height: 300px;
        width: 100%;
        object-fit: contain;
        border-radius: 0.75rem;
        box-shadow: 0 0.5rem 1rem rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
    }
</style>

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">
        Pull Requests (PRs) and Code Reviews are the backbone of collaborative software development. In Azure Repos, they help ensure that every piece of code entering your main branch has been reviewed, tested, and approved — keeping your project clean, stable, and maintainable.
    </p>
    <hr />

    <!-- What is a Pull Request -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What is a Pull Request?</h2>
        <p>
            A <strong>Pull Request</strong> is like raising your hand and saying, “I’ve written some code — can someone review it before we merge it into the main project?” It’s a safe and structured way to propose code changes, request feedback, and build better software together.
        </p>
        <p>Pull Requests allow teams to:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Collaborate on changes through comments and suggestions</li>
            <li>Ensure quality via code review workflows</li>
            <li>Keep the main branch stable and production-ready</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 1: Creating a PR -->
    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 1: Creating a Pull Request</h4>
            <p>Here’s how you create a PR in Azure Repos:</p>
            <ol>
                <li>Go to your repository inside the Azure DevOps project.</li>
                <li>Select <strong>Pull Requests</strong> from the left menu.</li>
                <li>Click <strong>New Pull Request</strong>.</li>
                <li>Choose your <strong>source branch</strong> (e.g., feature/login) and <strong>target branch</strong> (e.g., main).</li>
                <li>Enter a meaningful title and description explaining your changes.</li>
                <li>Tag reviewers — teammates who will look at your code.</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Create</strong> to send it off for review.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="~/css/Images/AzureDevOps/PullRequestCreation.jpg" alt="Creating a Pull Request" class="devops-img">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Submitting a pull request to review your changes.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Code Reviews -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What Happens During a Code Review?</h2>
        <p>
            Once your PR is submitted, reviewers step in to look over the code. This isn’t just about finding mistakes — it’s also about sharing knowledge, improving design, and learning from each other.
        </p>
        <p>During a code review, teammates can:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>View line-by-line code differences (also called a diff)</li>
            <li>Leave inline comments with suggestions or questions</li>
            <li>Approve the changes or request updates</li>
            <li>Rely on automated build and test checks for quick feedback</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2: Reviewing a PR -->
    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="~/css/Images/AzureDevOps/CodeReviewDevOps.png" alt="Code Review Comments" class="devops-img">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Team members provide feedback through comments and approvals.</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 2: Reviewing a Pull Request</h4>
            <p>
                As a reviewer, you’ll examine the changes and use the review tools to:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Comment on specific lines of code</li>
                <li>Start discussions or ask questions</li>
                <li>Approve the PR if everything looks good</li>
                <li>Request changes if improvements are needed</li>
                <li>Use automated checks to ensure everything builds and passes tests</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Merging -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Merging the Pull Request</h2>
        <p>
            After a PR is reviewed and approved by all required reviewers, it's time to merge! This means the proposed changes will officially be integrated into the main branch.
        </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3: Merging -->
    <div class="row align-items-center mt-4">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Step 3: Merging the Changes</h4>
            <p>
                Once the green light is given, follow these steps to complete the merge:
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>Click <strong>Complete Merge</strong> in the pull request view.</li>
                <li>You may choose to <strong>delete the feature branch</strong> (recommended to keep things clean).</li>
                <li>Click <strong>Confirm</strong> to merge the code into the target branch.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>
                Done! 🎉 Your code is now part of the main project — reviewed, validated, and ready to go.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="~/css/Images/AzureDevOps/MergePullDevOps.png" alt="Merging a Pull Request" class="devops-img">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Merging reviewed code into the main branch.</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Conclusion -->
    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            Pull Requests and Code Reviews bring out the best in teams. They improve quality, spark collaboration, and make sure every change to your codebase is thoughtful and peer-reviewed.
        </p>
        <p>
            In the next step, we’ll explore **Azure Boards**, where you'll learn how to organize your work with items like **Epics, Features, and User Stories**.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-start mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("CloningBranchingMerging", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">
            &larr; Previous: Cloning, Branching, and Merging
        </a>
    </div>
</div>
